Current list of drivers to be implemented and the status of re-implementation
=============================================================================

Guest packaging uses `rbuild kernel` / `rbuild kerneldrivers` with the profile
architecture. On the current ppc build box, only `drivers-ppc` optional drivers
are scanned; i386 projects are out of scope until an i386 profile run. WIP
paths live in `src/rbuild-1/kernel-drivers-blacklist.json` and are skipped until
promoted out of that list.

bus
All five bus drivers now build with `make` exit 0 for i386 and boot-test clean
under QEMU `-M pc`, each grafted one at a time into its own copy of the golden
image alongside the rebuilt i386 kernel. "Boot-tested" below means the kernel
reached its userland handoff (`Power management is enabled.`) with no panic,
trap or debugger entry on the serial console, and the run matched a control
boot of the same image with Apple's own driver in place. Retested 2026-09-20
against the current rebuilt kernel: all five now carry the boot past
`Configuring device drivers` and through to the Rhapsody Setup Assistant at
800x600, the five GUI frames pixel-identical to one another, and each driver
reproduces its documented behaviour below - PCI enumeration for drvPCIBus, the
duplicate PCMCIA0 for drvPCMCIABus, silent declines for Intel824X0 and PCIC.
The earlier note here that the machine never reached a login window, stalling at
`Configuring device drivers`, described the stock kernel, which in fact dies
earlier still in the open EIDE defect; it no longer holds. Controls with Apple's
own drivers were not re-run on that date. drvPCIBus and drvEISABus were also
booted together in one image, both binaries hash-verified in place beforehand:
they coexist, EISA0 registers, PCI enumeration still resolves the display at
bus 0 dev 2 func 0, and the desktop is pixel-identical to the PCIBus-only run.
None are hardware-tested.

 * drvEISABus - reconstructed against the reference binary, PnPDump 45/186 functions byte-identical with 96 hand-written leftovers accepted in the PnPDump ledger (compiler-shaped / stub / dual-bar), EISABus_reloc shared PnP class methods 53/93 identical or masked-eq; builds clean and boot-tested, registers EISA0 and survives a PnP BIOS that answers PNPB_R_FUNCTION_NOT_SUPPORTED. Retested 2026-09-20 to the Setup Assistant, and booted both alongside drvPCIBus and as part of the fully reconstructed stack with drvVGA. Diverges from Apple's binary in PnP read port (0x277 against Apple's 0x0)
 * drvPCIBus - reconstructed against the reference binary, fixes applied; its testIDs:dev:fun:bus: prototype now matches Apple's, and PCIResourceDriver inherits IODirectDevice; builds clean and boot-tested, registers PCI0 and enumerates every PCI device, serial output identical to the control. Retested 2026-09-20 to the Setup Assistant - the display resolves at bus 0 dev 2 func 0 through it - and booted both alongside drvEISABus and as part of the fully reconstructed stack with drvVGA
 * drvPCMCIABus - reconstructed against the reference binary, fixes applied; protocol records, every class instance size and ivar layout are byte-identical to the reference; two heap overflows from undeclared ivars fixed; builds clean and boot-tested. registers PCMCIA0 once and reaches the Setup Assistant. It previously registered PCMCIA0 twice; a 2026-09-20 control boot of Apple's own PCMCIABus_reloc registered once, and disassembly of the reference settled it - Apple sends registerDevice from initFromDeviceDescription: and its +probe: sends only alloc and initFromDeviceDescription:, where ours sent it from both. The duplicate was our defect, not an unexplained quirk of the reference, and removing the call from +probe: fixed it
 * Intel824X0PCI - reconstructed against the reference binary, fixes applied; builds clean and boot-tested. Loads and declines silently under QEMU, whose host bridge is 8086:1237 and not one of the 82434LX/NX IDs it auto-detects, so its probe path is untested; the 2026-09-20 retest reproduced the silent decline and still reached the Setup Assistant
 * Intel82365PCMCIA - reconstructed against the reference binary, all fixes applied; its five ObjC protocol records and four adoptions are byte-identical to the reference; builds clean and boot-tested, probes 0x3e0, reports no device and declines cleanly. QEMU has no PCMCIA bridge, so the attach path is untested; the 2026-09-20 retest reproduced the decline and still reached the Setup Assistant, but our PCIC_reloc is 42976 bytes against the golden image's 38912-byte slot, so injecting it needs a graft rather than an in-place write

ide
 * drvAHCI - newly written rather than reconstructed: Rhapsody predates AHCI, so no Apple reference binary exists. Tested working - boots to userland under QEMU q35 on the built-in ICH9 SATA controller (8086:2922), with both ports detecting their disks, hd0/hd1 registering and root mounting over AHCI. Needs the UEFI loader's PCI config-mechanism report to bind; Not yet tested on hardware
 * drvEIDE - refactored, now with VIA, AMD, and Intel PIIX to ICH4 specific code. First build attempted 2026-09-20 with `rbuild buildpackage --arch i386`; it had never been compiled and needed four fixes to get there - an accidental `*/` inside a PIIX.h comment, a C89 declaration-after-statement in IdeCnt.m that the refactor's own _pollMode early return introduced and which broke both arms of the NO_IRQ_MSG conditional, a missing IdeCntInit.h import in IdeDisk.m that made a struct-returning method default to id, and a missing IODevice.h import in DualEide.m that left IO_DRIVERKIT_VERSION undefined so the pre-DriverKit-4.0 bus lookup compiled instead of the modern one Apple's binary uses. It packages as drveide-33-i386.apk. The incomplete IdeController(PIIX) category is fixed - the refactor moved bmInitPRDTable, bmRegisterRange:, performATAPIDMA:buffer:client: and performDMA:taskfile:command: into IdeController(BMIDE) in IdeBMIDE.m but left their declarations in the PIIX @interface; they now sit in their own BMIDE @interface and the build is warning-clean. Boot-tested working 2026-09-21 under QEMU: hc0 detects the controller at 0x1f0 irq 14, identifies the drive, negotiates the cable and UDMA mode, registers hc0 and hd0, reports geometry and capacity, and the root filesystem mounts on /dev/hd0a through it to a full userland. Getting there took a booter fix, not a driver fix - see docs/boot/sarld-driver-link-limit.md. The driver requires this tree's kernel: it references seven _ata_hd_* symbols that our mach_kernel exports and Apple's does not. Not hardware-tested
 * drvPCFloppy - reconstructed against Apple's Floppy_reloc; not yet tested on hardware

input
 * drvBusMouse - 7/11 hand-written functions masked-equal to BusMouse_reloc, apple-generic version objects emitted. Its Makefile was the only one in the tree that left DriverHelp out of LOCAL_RESOURCES, so the directory was never copied into the build and driver.make's movehelp died renaming a directory that was not there; the package builds once it is declared. Boot-tested 2026-09-21 under QEMU, which has no bus mouse: it probes, reports "No bus mouse installed", declines, and the machine boots on to the Setup Assistant - the same lines the stock driver produces, so the probe path itself is still untested. BusMouse_reloc is 29664 bytes against Apple's 29796. Not hardware-tested
 * drvISASerialPort - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvPCParallel - reconstructed against the reference binary, 40/75 reloc functions byte-identical, 17 masked-equal, remainder accepted compiler-shaped. Needed two build fixes: its Kernel Server declared IOParallelPort.h and IOParallelPortKern.h as public headers with no install directory, and its Makefile.postamble duplicated an ofile the default already held. Boot-tested working 2026-09-21 under QEMU with an emulated LPT port (-parallel null): it probes, detects the port and registers pp0; with the port removed it declines and pp0 never appears, so the detection is real and not unconditional. ParallelPort_reloc is 45404 bytes against Apple's 45312. A third fix made the package complete: the PostLoad and PreLoad tools name the startup object explicitly, because the host's own /lib/crt1.o is ppc-only, and c3f25ae20 had pointed them at an absolute /build/bootstrap-root on the build host. rbuild builds inside a chroot that has no /build, so that path did not exist there and both tools failed to link; the chroot root already supplies a fat /lib/crt1.o, /usr/lib and /System/Library/Frameworks, so the prefix is now empty and overridable. The package carries ParallelPort_reloc, RemovePPDev (17240 bytes against Apple's 17272) and InstallPPDev (37812 against 37364), all i386. Not hardware-tested
 * drvPS2Keyboard - reconstructed against the reference binary, 34/47 hand-written functions byte-identical under relocation masking, remainder accepted as compiler-shaped. Needed a DriverInfo file, which the project never had, before it would package. Boot-tested working 2026-09-21 under QEMU: registers PS2Controller and PCKeyboard0 as a boot driver, and typing y at the kernel's "Continue without network?" prompt is echoed and taken, which carries the boot through to the Setup Assistant desktop - that keystroke goes through the loaded driver, not the booter's BIOS input. PS2Keyboard_reloc is 43408 bytes against Apple's 43460. Not hardware-tested
 * drvPS2Mouse - reconstructed against the reference binary, 6/11 hand-written functions masked-equal to PS2Mouse_reloc, five compiler-shaped leftovers accepted. Needed a DriverInfo file and the removal of its Kernel Server Makefile.postamble, whose OTHER_GENERATED_OFILES += $(VERS_OFILE) appended a second copy of an entry the default value already held; once the guest started resolving its versioning system that duplicate reached kl_ld and ld refused it, so no _reloc was produced at all. Boot-tested working 2026-09-21 under QEMU with PS2Mouse added to Active Drivers: registers PS2Mouse, the event system stops reporting "Can't find active pointer device", and injected relative motion moves the cursor on the desktop - 182 pixels changed against 0 for the same run with no pointer driver. Movement is scaled by the event system rather than one-to-one, but proportional on both axes. PS2Mouse_reloc is 30212 bytes against Apple's 30204. SGS VERS_STRING/VERS_NUM are still unmet and cannot be met here; apple-generic versioning emits VersionString/VersionNumber instead. Not hardware-tested
 * drvSerialPointingDevice - reconstructed against the reference binary, 10/16 hand-written functions byte-identical under relocation masking, remainder accepted as compiler-shaped. Needed three build fixes: a DriverInfo file it never had, removal of a Makefile.postamble that duplicated an ofile the default already held, and its help directory renamed from Help to DriverHelp so movehelp could produce the Help the reference ships. Boot-tested 2026-09-21 under QEMU with a Microsoft serial mouse on COM1 (-serial msmouse): it loads, acquires ISASerialPort0, reads its config and runs the reset handshake three times, but receives no identification bytes ("Listening ... {}") and reports no mouse. Apple's stock SerialPointingDevice_reloc prints byte-identical output on the same setup, so this is QEMU's msmouse not answering the RTS/DTR reset rather than a divergence - the driver matches the reference, and the detect path remains unproven either way. SerialPointingDevice_reloc is 40048 bytes against Apple's 39928. Not hardware-tested

network
 * drvAMDPCNet32II - complete
 * drvDEC21X4X - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvDEC21142 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvDECchip21040 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvDECchip21140 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvEtherExpress16 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvEtherLink3 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvEtherLinkXL - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvIntel1000 - not a reconstruction: ported from the third-party onionmixer/openstep-intel1000 (BSD-2-Clause), wired up here as a Driver bundle/Kernel Server pair with apk/pkginfo. Covers the PCI/PCI-X 8254x gigabit family; its author verified it on real 82547EI hardware, the rest of the family only against the SDM and FreeBSD's e1000 code. Needs compiled and then tested here
 * drvIntel82556 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvIntelE100 - new, not a reconstruction: an original BSD-2-Clause driver for the Intel 8255x (e100) 10/100 family - 82557, 82558, 82559, 82559ER, 82550, 82551 and the ICH2-ICH7 integrated parts - written from Intel's 8255x Open Source Software Developer Manual, with FreeBSD's if_fxp consulted for the device IDs, the EEPROM map and the errata (see its NOTICE); it transmits in flexible mode with one buffer descriptor and does not rely on the RFD EOF bit, as FreeBSD's if_fxp does not, and the installed ar7-branch QEMU's simplified-mode transmit sends zero-length frames and never sets it. It replaces drvIntel82557, the decompiled Apple driver, which is deleted. Its plain-C logic and register access have unit tests in tests/, run on the build guest by vm/build-i386-e100.sh. Boot-tested on 2026-09-23 under QEMU with vm/e100-boot.py on i82557b, i82559er, i82801 and QEMU's i82551 model (8086:1209 rev 0f, driven as the 82550/82551 generation), each on its own image: every one identifies the part, reads the MAC with a good EEPROM checksum, finds the PHY and reports 100Mb/s full duplex; in single-user mode en0 pings 10.0.2.2 with frames both ways in the host-side capture, and a normal boot reaches the Setup Assistant. With an NE2000 in place of the card, +probe: rejects it before registering an interface, so the NE2000 comes up as en0. On real hardware, Instance0.table's "IRQ Levels" must be set to the card's IRQ. Known limits: duplex follows the PHY's FDX pin, so an 82557 with a DP83840 that does not wire it runs half duplex; Dynamic Standby left enabled in an ICH2/82559 EEPROM is worked around, never cleared; the simplified receive path on the 82550/82551 is proven only against QEMU. Not hardware-tested
 * drvIntel82595 - needs compiled and then tested
 * drvIntel82596
 * drvNE2k - complete
 * drvSMC16 - complete
 * drvSMCUltra - complete
 * drvVMwareVMXNet - complete

